[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-38", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 38 — the HTTP layer is not the wall (16k no-op/s); G1 marked below the live set; 64 slots 4233 ops/s", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-38.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 38 - the HTTP layer is not the wall; G1 was marking continuously below the live set\n\n> Seed: iteration 37's S29. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2; rung :sf1-full;\n> SUT levi (10 cores, 16 threads, -Xmx10g), driver judah, LAN.\n\n## Generate / Rank\n\n  1. S29, the HTTP layer's cost per request. A `:noop` op through the\n     same route, EDN parse and response shape (an unknown op is refused\n     with 400 -- control shown). Prediction: 8-12k ops/s; at or below 5k\n     the HTTP layer is the wall. Observed: 16,064.8 ops/s at 64 slots\n     with 100% on-time and the sweep still passing; wire p50 1.7 ms.\n     Neither the JDK HttpServer nor the single-threaded node driver is\n     the wall at 3.9k real ops/s. Held.\n\n  2. Where the CPU goes, then. jstat on the SUT during a 64-slot run:\n     GC PAUSES 0.30 s in 26 s -- not it. But four concurrent marking\n     cycles in 26 s with old-gen occupancy at 90%: the default adaptive\n     IHOP started marking below the 5 GB live set, so G1 was marking\n     continuously on other cores -- load 13 on 10 cores while server-ms\n     accounted for ~5. With -XX:-G1UseAdaptiveIHOP\n     -XX:InitiatingHeapOccupancyPercent=75 (marking starts above the live\n     set): 6 cycles in 90 s, load 8 at the same rate.\n\n    rung sf1-full  slots  threads  reads/s  upd/s  on-time%  thru/s\n      it.37          64     16      2560     1475   100.00    3852.3\n      it.38          64     16      2944     1475   100.00    4233.3   (3072: 85.9% fails; was 48% before IHOP)\n\n  Read-after-write 360/0/0. Gate 1,082/0.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - The cheap measurement (a no-op route, 5 minutes) removed a whole\n    class of next steps -- rewriting the server -- before anyone wrote\n    one. The jstat sample then named the actual consumer of the missing\n    cores. Both were 'measure before deciding'; neither was a guess.\n  - At 64 slots the wire p50 of a 0.2 ms op is 10.8 ms: 64 in flight\n    over 16 threads is ~9 ms of queueing in the SUT. The column is\n    CPU-bound on this host; the next lever is either more cores (M4) or\n    a CPU profile of the engine under the mix (S30) -- IC09's 2,228-group\n    expansion and IC07's fetch-more loop are the known heavy ops.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: GROWTH (64-slot column 3852 ->\n4233 on the same rung, mix and threads; a JVM flag stated in the row).\nRatio to the audited No.1: 33.\n\nSeeds for iteration 39, in order:\n\n  - S30: a CPU profile of the engine under the mix (which functions, not\n    which ops).\n  - M4: more cores for the SUT.\n  - S26: a full-data parameter set. S23b: IC05. M3: SF3 (~13 GB).\n"}]
